Feature Request: Banking-Grade PIN Authentication for QGuard Messaging (Android)
Objective

Implement a secure application PIN authentication system for the QGuard Messaging Android application, similar to the authentication experience provided by modern banking applications.

User Flow:

First-Time Login
The user installs and opens the QGuard Messaging app.
The user signs in using their email and password (or their existing authentication method).
After successful authentication:
Register the device as a trusted device.
Store the authentication session securely.
Prompt the user to create an application PIN.
Allow the user to choose either:
4-digit PIN
6-digit PIN
Ask the user to confirm the PIN before saving it.
Returning User Experience

Once the device has been registered:

The user should NOT be required to enter their email and password every time they open the application.
Instead, every app launch should display a secure PIN entry screen.
After entering the correct PIN:
Unlock the application.
Restore the authenticated session.
Navigate directly to the user's conversations/dashboard.

This should behave similarly to banking applications where users log in only once and subsequently unlock the app using their PIN.

Security Requirements
Secure PIN Storage

Never store the PIN in plaintext.

Use Android security best practices:

Android Keystore
EncryptedSharedPreferences or Jetpack Security Crypto
Hardware-backed encryption when available

The PIN must be encrypted before storage.

Session Authentication

After the initial successful login:

Store authentication tokens securely.
Maintain the authenticated session.
The PIN should only unlock the locally stored authenticated session.
The PIN must not replace server authentication.
Tokens should still expire according to backend policy.
When tokens expire or become invalid:
Require the user to authenticate again using email and password.
After successful authentication, continue using the existing PIN.
Failed PIN Protection

Implement banking-style protection:

Maximum of 5 incorrect PIN attempts.
After 5 failed attempts:
Lock the application.
Require full authentication using email and password.
Clear failed attempt counter after successful authentication.
Additional Security Features

Implement the following:

Auto-lock after configurable inactivity (10 minutes).
Lock the app whenever it goes into the background.
Require the PIN when reopening the app.
Prevent screenshots on sensitive screens using FLAG_SECURE.
Clear sensitive UI from the Recent Apps preview.
Detect rooted devices if supported by the existing security architecture.
Prevent PIN brute-force attacks using increasing delays between failed attempts.
PIN Management

Inside Settings → Security, allow users to:

Change PIN
Remove PIN (requires password verification)
Switch between 4-digit and 6-digit PIN
Enable or disable PIN authentication
Configure auto-lock timeout
Reset forgotten PIN (requires email/password authentication)
Biometric Authentication (Optional but Recommended)

Support Android Biometrics:

Fingerprint
Face Unlock
Device Credentials

Workflow:

User enters PIN once.
Enable biometric authentication.
Future app launches may use biometrics.
If biometrics fail, fall back to the PIN.
Device Registration

Each trusted device should have:

Unique Device ID
Device Name
Registration Timestamp
Last Login Timestamp
Platform Information
App Version

Allow users to manage trusted devices from their account settings.

Logout Behavior

When the user logs out:

Delete locally stored authentication tokens.
Delete the encrypted PIN.
Remove the trusted device registration.
Require full email/password login again.
Enterprise Security Goals

The implementation should follow Android security best practices and provide a banking-grade user experience with:

Secure encrypted credential storage
Trusted device registration
Session persistence
PIN-based application unlocking
Secure token lifecycle management
Protection against brute-force attacks
Hardware-backed cryptography where available
Clean architecture and production-ready implementation

The authentication flow should feel seamless: users authenticate with their email and password only once on a trusted device, then unlock the app securely using their PIN (or biometrics) on subsequent launches, while preserving enterprise-grade security throughout the authentication lifecycle.